{
  "id": "Compound::DB06637 - Gene::3784",
  "verbalization": "Dalfampridine is shown to interact with multiple potassium channel subunits, specifically Kcna3, Kcnd1, and Kcnc3, all of which are involved in the regulation of outward and delayed rectifier potassium currents. These channels are functionally linked to the activity of Kcnq1, a key component of the delayed rectifier potassium current. The binding of Dalfampridine to these channels suggests a potential mechanism by which it may modulate the activity of Kcnq1, potentially influencing cellular potassium homeostasis. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, biologically plausible relationship between Dalfampridine and Kcnq1, supported by the shared involvement of these channels in potassium current regulation. The mechanism appears to involve the modulation of potassium channel activity, which could have implications for cellular function and disease states involving abnormal potassium signaling.",
